One major challenge of the latter array assemblies of SKAO, namely AA* and AA4, is reconstructing images from the enormous amounts of data that will be produced. This can be tackled through scaleable software pipelines that employ parellelization, typically along the time and frequency axes. We propose a framework, within the traditional major-minor cycle image reconstruction paradigm, that allows for parallelization by another axis, namely, baseline length. It achieves this by creating pseudo-full-resolution problems for each node by using the locally stored visibilities together with previous major-cycle reconstructed images from the other nodes. We demonstrate our framework's generality by applying it to both multi-scale CLEAN and sparsity-regularized convex reconstruction, and evaluate its effectiveness by comparing these, in the context of two partitions, against the reconstruction methods' serial counterparts across a variety of simulated and real datasets. We found that the parallelization framework allows for significantly increased reconstruction times for images of similar quality, particularly in the case of our larger datasets where we were able to achieve close to a $2\times$ speedup. These promising results motivate further study of the framework, with the aims of providing more flexibility and options for designing scaleable imaging pipelines for radio-interferometric data.